<!-- 01 — CONCEPT -->
<section class="container measure section-block section-block--tight">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">01 — Concept</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">The concept as the single binding source</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Every mandate starts with the same question: what does the market position actually have to achieve — and does the existing communication still carry it? The strategic concept emerges from the answer, never the other way round. Design, choice of channel and execution follow from the concept, not from a preference for a particular format.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">This concept is the single binding source for everything that follows — whether it is an advertisement, a video, a website or an automatically generated product description.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Why the sequence matters</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">If the design comes first and the rationale is looked for afterwards, contradictions arise that become visible at the latest with the second channel or the second market. A logo created without a conceptual foundation cannot be underpinned with a strategy retrospectively — the strategy has to be in place first, so that design, copy and choice of channel flow from the same source instead of contradicting each other.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">What goes into the concept</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Three areas of knowledge go into every concept before a single design decision is made:</p>
  <ul class="check-list" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
    {%- for item in [
      "Industry knowledge: how the market actually works, not how it looks from outside",
      "Strategic understanding: which decision actually changes the market position",
      "Market know-how: how manufacturers, distributors and end customers each assess an offer differently"
    ] %}
    <li class="check-item reveal reveal-delay-{{ loop.index0 % 3 }}">
      <span class="check-icon"></span>
      <span>{{ item }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- 02 — FROM THE END -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">02 — From the end</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">It starts with the moment of decision</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Most communication projects start with the means: a campaign, a website, a new brochure. The tool is fixed before the task is clear. Here the order is reversed. The starting point is the moment in which someone decides — and only from there does it follow, backwards, what is needed.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Four questions describe that moment:</p>
  <ul class="check-list" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
    {%- for item in [
      "Who actually decides? Not who signs, but who prepares the decision — and who can overturn it.",
      "Where, and within what time? Three seconds at the chiller cabinet is a different task from a six-month tender process.",
      "Against which alternative? “Doing nothing” is an alternative too — often the strongest.",
      "What does this person know at this moment, and what do they believe? The gap between the two is the real task."
    ] %}
    <li class="check-item reveal reveal-delay-{{ loop.index0 % 3 }}">
      <span class="check-icon"></span>
      <span>{{ item }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">That is why what will be produced is not fixed when a mandate starts. Sometimes it is a pack, sometimes a single sentence — and sometimes the realisation that the commissioned means is not the right one. Saying so is part of the work.</p>
</section>

<!-- 03 — BACKGROUND -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">03 — Background</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Thought through to the end</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Agencies usually come from design or business administration – and deliver exactly what clients expect of them there. Lünstroth adds a third perspective.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">This agency grew from a different direction. Its founder comes from law, philosophy, ethnology and political science and lectures at the German Federal Patent Court. The result is a way of working that thinks a question through on a scientific basis and from a broader cultural perspective – to the end: What does a sign mean in another culture? What holds up legally? What do people believe, and why? The same approach shapes the <a href="https://luenstroth.com/en/">Lünstroth</a> brand consultancy.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The heads of the individual disciplines have, in addition, studied their field with distinction. Every mandate thus combines professional excellence with a broad perspective.</p>
</section>

<!-- 04 — CREDIBILITY -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">04 — Credibility</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Being believed instead of being liked</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A sentence that sounds right can be produced in seconds today. A sentence that is believed cannot. Credibility comes from decisions about content: from information people can check, from statements that stay the same over years, and from the courage to leave out what cannot be backed up.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">On the Delikato salad range, the fat content is printed on the pack variety by variety — even where the figure is less flattering. Information that appears only when it is favourable is advertising. Information that always appears is a statement of fact.</p>

  <figure class="reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin:2.5rem auto;max-width:520px;">
    <img src="/img/volksbank-lippstadt-luftbild-2011.webp" alt="Full-page newspaper advertisement for Volksbank Lippstadt: aerial photo of the town with the branch locations marked, headline in German: “Of course you can bank online with Volksbank. But you can also just walk round the corner.”" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="1769" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
    <figcaption style="font-size:.75rem;color:rgba(var(--ink-rgb),.5);margin-top:.6rem;line-height:1.5;">Volksbank Lippstadt, daily newspaper advertisement, 2011. Aerial photo: digital orthophoto, flight of 27.04.2008. Data source: State of NRW / Geobasis NRW, <a href="https://www.govdata.de/dl-de/by-2-0" rel="license noopener" target="_blank" style="color:inherit;">dl-de/by-2-0</a>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">“We are there for you locally” appears in every other bank advertisement. This one does not claim proximity, it reveals it: an aerial photo of the town with every branch in it. Whoever opens the paper looks for their own street first — and answers the question of distance themselves.</p>
  <p class="pull-quote reveal reveal-delay-1">A draft can please everyone and move no one.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Emotion is welcome – in confectionery, sport and fashion it is the product. But being liked is no measure of it: approval in the meeting says nothing about whether anything happens in the market. Conversely, the aerial photo above is not a pretty image. It moves people anyway, because readers find their own street in it.</p>
</section>

<!-- 05 — BRAND FILTER -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">05 — Brand filter</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">A rulebook that machines understand too</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The concept gives rise to a documented brand filter: clear rules for tone of voice, terminology, visual limits and permissible claims. A brand filter written only for human creatives is no longer enough.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">More and more companies use AI systems themselves — in customer service, in automated content creation, in marketing automation. These systems communicate on behalf of the brand, whether that was planned or not. A brand filter must therefore be documented so precisely that it not only guides our own work, but can also serve as a guardrail for the client's own AI systems.</p>
  <p class="pull-quote reveal reveal-delay-2">A brand that is only consistent as long as a human checks every single statement is not really consistent at its core.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal" style="margin-top:1rem;">What a brand filter actually contains</h3>
  <ul class="check-list" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
    {%- for item in [
      "Tone-of-voice rules: which tone fits the brand and which does not, with concrete examples instead of abstract adjectives",
      "Terminology glossary: which terms for products, target groups and services are binding",
      "Positive and negative examples: phrasings that carry the brand and phrasings that undermine it",
      "Visual limits: how much design freedom exists and where it ends"
    ] %}
    <li class="check-item reveal reveal-delay-{{ loop.index0 % 3 }}">
      <span class="check-icon"></span>
      <span>{{ item }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Why this matters for the client's AI systems in particular</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">An AI customer-service chatbot that draws on outdated terminology, or an automated product description that makes a claim that does not fit the current positioning, undermines a brand's consistency just as effectively as a flawed advertisement — only at a much higher rate and without a human checking every single output. A brand filter that is explicitly documented for use in such systems reduces this risk structurally, instead of leaving it to the attention of individuals.</p>
</section>

<!-- 06 — ADAPTATION -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">06 — Adaptation</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">The dream of the common thread — and the reality of large organisations</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A concept that runs seamlessly and unchanged through every country, every department and every sales partner is the right ambition, strategically and in communication — and in the practice of large companies almost never the reality. Entrenched silos, different priorities between regions, internal responsibilities that cannot be dissolved by decree: a concept that ignores this fails in the client's own organisation before it ever reaches the end customer.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">That is why every concept is developed from the outset so that it can adapt — to different international conditions and to different price tiers within the same portfolio, without losing its underlying substance. Not a rigid rulebook that has to look identical everywhere, but a robust core that can respond to the actual situation on the ground.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Where the common thread breaks in practice</h3>
  <ul class="check-list" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
    {%- for item in [
      "Regional sales structures with marketing organisations at different stages of maturity",
      "Linguistic and cultural differences that make a literal translation useless",
      "Different competitive situations in each market that call for a different weighting of the same message",
      "Special arrangements of individual national subsidiaries, grown over years, that will not disappear overnight"
    ] %}
    <li class="check-item reveal reveal-delay-{{ loop.index0 % 3 }}">
      <span class="check-icon"></span>
      <span>{{ item }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">What can be adapted — and what cannot</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Not every element of a concept is equally binding. The underlying positioning and the central message remain unchanged — they are the core that no regional adaptation may dilute. Wording, detailed imagery, channel weighting and price communication, on the other hand, may and must adapt to the respective market reality. This separation is documented from the start instead of being renegotiated with every deviation.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Price tiers as a concrete example</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A product positioned in the premium segment in its home market may have to compete in a mid-price tier in another market — for competitive reasons, because of local purchasing power, or because of an established sales partner with its own pricing logic. The brand message remains recognisably the same; the price communication and the argument for why the price is justified differ deliberately. That is not a dilution of the concept but the precondition for it to work in the respective price tier at all.</p>
</section>

<!-- 07 — EXECUTION -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">07 — Execution</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">From approval to roll-out</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Once a concept has been approved, the mandate does not end with handing over templates. Lünstroth handles production entirely in-house — for example social media video clips that are produced, not merely conceived, in up to <span class="stat">20 languages</span>.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Who handles the roll-out</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The actual placement — when and where finished material is published — usually lies with the client, but can be taken over on request. The same applies to social media: concept, content and production lie with Lünstroth; day-to-day operation — posting, community management — with the client.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Media buying: different by sector</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">In B2C fashion, Lünstroth buys advertising space as well as TV and radio airtime itself. In B2B, media buying has so far remained with the client — a deliberate distinction grounded in the respective market practice, not a blanket service.</p>

  <h3 class="subhead reveal">Fine-tuning after launch</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Performance monitoring and iterative adjustment after publication are usually handled by the client's own marketing team. We offer this too on request.</p>
</section>

